    "created_at": "2025-04-22T04:18:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-04-22T04:23:37Z",
    "body": "This shadowrootreferencetarget idea seems too limited:\n\n- it's like a pipe where only one thing fits, but we want multiple:\n  - someone outside of the root might want to reference multiple things inside the root, as if each one is a separate wire but this feature allows only a single wire.\n- it is an improper inversion of control:\n  - If someone on the outside (of a 3rd party custom element for example) wants to target something in the root, and someone else also on the outside wants to target something else, not only is it unreasonable for the 3rd party to update their code so that the specific users can target what they want, it is not possible to satisfy both users.\n  - if I *need* to target something in a custom element for this sort of thing, I wouldn't want to be blocked by the CE author.\n\nIt seems like we need shadow piercing selectors for this to be doable in a way where, at least with 3rd party components with that may have more than one targetable thing in it, it would *just work*. \n\nFor example, imagine a 3rd party element with more than one `<input>` in it. Now what?\n\nEven if it was `<cool-input>` with a single `input` in it, a higher level `cool-form` component might have multiple `cool-inputs` in it, both by the same author, and the user (or multiple users) outside of the `cool-form` will be totally stuck, unable to target anything.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
